  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Issued in an envelope resembling those used for developed pictures by photo developers for their customers. Sixteen photographic prints of illustrative drawings (one is all writing) with hand-written explanations, 10 x 15 cm in size. Accompanied by a sheet of bright orange with a description of the project.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">16 fotos que no tomé reflects on some fundamentals of photography and the mechanics of memory, while it brings together, with childish impertinence, two artistic genres that appear to be opposites: drawing and photography. The European edition of the work (it has already been reissued three times) was published by Poursuite Editions. This first edition in Spanish by the Laboratorio Editorial C'est un livre proposes not only to translate Grimalt's explanations, but to translate as well the original form of a book into that of a device that is inherently linked to its content. This Mexican version also has three photo-drawings generated expressly for the Latin American public that don't appear in the French editions.</subfield>
  </datafield>
